 Any is the root of the Kotlin class hierarchy.
It is similar to Object in Java.
Because everything is a child of Any, 
every object possesses three specific functions defined 
in Any: 
1.      equals(): To check if two objects are the same.
2.      hashCode(): To give the object a numerical ID.
3.      toString(): To convert the object into text. This is why you can call toString() on absolutely anything in Kotlin,
even if you didn't write that function yourself.
You inherited it! 
You can override these standard functions too. 

class Car(val brand: String) {
    override fun toString(): String {
        return "A beautiful $brand car"
    }
}
 fun main() {
    val c = Car("Toyota")
    println(c) // Automatically calls toString()
}
   Output: A beautiful Toyota car
